  "preview": "A vet tech counts 42 puppy heartbeats in 18 seconds. The owner wants bpm. The vet wants beats per hour. Can the kids find both, and write a rule?",
  "body": {
    "goal": "Build a proportional relationship from a single measurement, find the constant of proportionality in different units, write the rule y = kx, and reason about reasonableness.",
    "studentPrompt": "Dr. Patel is the veterinarian at Maple Lane Animal Clinic. She places a stethoscope on a sleeping golden retriever puppy and counts 42 heartbeats in 18 seconds.\n\n1. Complete a table that shows the puppy's heartbeat count for these times: 18 s, 30 s, 1 min, 1.5 min, 5 min, and 1 hour. Assume the heart rate stays constant.\n\n2. What's the constant of proportionality in beats per minute (bpm)? In beats per second? In beats per hour? Show how those three numbers are related.\n\n3. Write an equation y = kx where x is time in MINUTES and y is the total number of heartbeats.\n\n4. A healthy resting heart rate for a puppy this age is 120-160 bpm. Is this puppy in the healthy range? Defend your answer.\n\n5. Dr. Patel counts the heart rate of an adult dog in the next room and records 24 beats in 15 seconds. Which dog has the faster heart rate, and by how many bpm?",
    "image": {
      "kind": "url",
      "url": "https://images.pexels.com/photos/6235020/pexels-photo-6235020.jpeg?auto=compress&cs=tinysrgb&h=650&w=940",
      "alt": "Veterinarian consults a woman with her Pomeranian in a monochrome clinic setting.",
      "credit": "Photo by Tima Miroshnichenko on Pexels"
    },
    "launch": "Tell the story: 'Dr. Patel counts 42 beats in 18 seconds. The puppy's owner is in the room asking what the pulse is. What do you tell her?' That's it — send them to the boards. Don't hand them a table yet.",
    "materials": [
      "Vertical whiteboards",
      "Markers",
      "Calculator (optional — encourage mental math first)"
    ],
    "concepts": [
      "proportional-reasoning",
      "constant-of-proportionality",
      "unit-rate",
      "equation-y=kx",
      "unit-conversion",
      "real-world-application"
    ],
    "taskType": "anchor",
    "btcFit": "Genuine anchor task. Concrete entry (anyone can scale 42 in 18s to 60s). The 'k in three units' move forces students to notice that k is not a single number — it's a number plus a unit. Q5 is a unit-rate comparison disguised as a clinical question.",
    "anticipatedApproaches": "- Scale-up: 42 in 18s, so in 60s it's 42 × (60/18) = 42 × 10/3 = 140 bpm.\n- Unit rate first: 42/18 ≈ 2.33 beats per second, then × 60 = 140 bpm.\n- Doubling and adding: 42 in 18s → 84 in 36s → close to 140 in 60s (less precise but valid).\n- Strong groups notice the three k-values are related by × 60 (sec→min→hour): ~2.33 bps, 140 bpm, 8,400 bph.\n- For Q5: groups compute the adult's rate as 24 × 4 = 96 bpm and subtract.",
    "commonMisconceptions": "- Multiplying 42 × 60 / 18 wrong (e.g., 42 × 60 = 2520, then dividing wrong). Redirect: 'What does 42/18 mean in words?'\n- Saying k is just '140' without units. Redirect: 'If I told my friend k = 140, would that mean per second, per minute, or per hour? You have to tell me.'\n- For Q3, writing y = 42x or y = 18x — using one of the original numbers instead of the unit rate.\n- For Q5, comparing 42 vs. 24 directly and concluding the puppy is faster 'because 42 > 24' — ignoring that the time windows are different.",
    "discussionQuestions": [
      "Three groups got three different numbers for k (2.33, 140, 8400) but everyone says they're right. How can that be?",
      "If we measured the puppy's heart for only 5 seconds and got 12 beats, would the bpm we calculate be MORE or LESS reliable than the 18-second count? Why?",
      "In your equation y = kx, what happens to k if you change x from minutes to hours? What stays the same?"
    ],
    "extensions": [
      "Dr. Patel's pulse monitor only reports a whole number of bpm. If the true rate is between 139.5 and 140.5 bpm, what's the smallest time window she could count for and still trust the displayed number? (Hint: how many beats does she need in her count?)",
      "A vet student suggests an even faster method: count for 6 seconds and multiply by 10. What's the trade-off? Pick two different 'true' bpm values (one healthy, one not) and show how a miscount of even one beat changes the answer.",
      "The adult dog from Q5 has a heart rate of 96 bpm. A human adult's resting heart rate is 60-100 bpm. Build a single bar graph or number line comparing the puppy, the adult dog, and a typical human. Whose heart works hardest per minute? Per LIFETIME (look up dog vs. human life span and estimate total heartbeats)?"
    ],
    "sampleSolutions": "1. Rate: 42 / 18 = 7/3 beats per second.\n   18 s → 42 beats\n   30 s → 70 beats\n   1 min (60 s) → 140 beats\n   1.5 min (90 s) → 210 beats\n   5 min (300 s) → 700 beats\n   1 hour (3600 s) → 8,400 beats\n2. k = 7/3 beats per second ≈ 2.33 bps; k = 140 bpm; k = 8,400 beats per hour. They are related by ×60 each step (60 s = 1 min, 60 min = 1 hr).\n3. y = 140x (x in minutes, y in beats).\n4. 140 bpm falls inside the healthy 120-160 bpm range. The puppy is healthy.\n5. Adult dog: 24 beats / 15 s = 1.6 bps × 60 = 96 bpm. Puppy is faster by 140 − 96 = 44 bpm.",
